# ALLOWLIST. Everything is ignored unless named below.
#
# This is deliberately inverted. A denylist protects only the paths you thought of, and
# would publish the next working directory the moment it was called something new, along
# with any brief or requirements document left in the repo root.
#
# pica ships the METHOD: rules, commands, hooks, scripts, the sector base, the checks.
# Working material is not content for anyone installing it, and it carries briefs, real
# copy and identifiers that are not redistributable. What survives it belongs in
# CHANGELOG.md, which is the durable form: a rule stated next to the failure that earned
# it, and nothing that identifies where the failure happened.
#
# To publish something new, add it here on purpose. If you find yourself adding a
# directory of work rather than a directory of method, that is the signal to stop.

/*

# ---- the plugin surface: this is what pica IS ----
!/.claude-plugin/
!/commands/
!/hooks/
!/packages/
!/skills/
!/scripts/

# ---- repo furniture ----
!/README.md
!/CHANGELOG.md
!/LICENSE
!/.gitignore

# ---- README artwork, authored here and containing no project content ----
# SVG only, deliberately: it is text, so it diffs and reviews like source. Raster
# formats stay ignored below precisely because a screenshot is the easiest way to
# leak a client's screen into the repo without noticing.
!/assets/
/assets/*
!/assets/*.svg

# ---- docs: design specs and implementation plans, never brainstorm scratch ----
!/docs/
/docs/*
!/docs/specs/
!/docs/plans/

# ---- belt and braces ----
# These are ignored at ANY depth, including inside an allowlisted directory, so a
# captured artefact or a client brief cannot ride along inside skills/ or docs/.
.pica/
.audit/
.cmp/
node_modules/
*.docx
*.pdf
*.png
*.jpg
*.jpeg
*.sketch
*.fig
